    <span class="section-label">Honest comparison</span>
    <h1>Squarespace vs. <em>a custom-coded site.</em></h1>
    <p>Squarespace is the prettiest of the major builders. That is also why this comparison is the most nuanced: for some businesses Squarespace really is the right answer, and I will not pretend otherwise. Here is the honest tradeoff.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container" style="max-width:980px">

    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Where Squarespace genuinely wins</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Visual design is good out of the box.</strong> Squarespace templates are nicer than Wix templates. If your business is design-led (photographer, restaurant, boutique retail, wedding venue), the platform is built for that aesthetic.</li>
      <li><strong>Editing is straightforward.</strong> The editor is cleaner than Wix's, less click-heavy than WordPress's. If you plan to edit weekly, Squarespace is one of the friendlier loops.</li>
      <li><strong>Built-in commerce that actually works.</strong> If you are selling products, Squarespace's e-commerce is competent. Custom-coded marketing sites can integrate Stripe / Shopify, but for a fully integrated store on day one, Squarespace is faster.</li>
      <li><strong>You want one bill, one platform.</strong> Domain + hosting + email + commerce + analytics all under Squarespace's umbrella. For an owner who does not want to coordinate multiple vendors, that is a real benefit (it is also a real cost &mdash; see below).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where Squarespace hurts you</h2>
    <p>Squarespace's strengths come with structural costs. Some of them are visible only after a year or two of use.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Page speed is mediocre.</strong> Squarespace sites usually score 60&ndash;75 on Google PageSpeed mobile. Better than Wix, still well behind a custom build at 95&ndash;100. Their template editor sacrifices speed for flexibility &mdash; every page loads the full editor runtime even though your visitors do not need it.</li>
      <li><strong>Local SEO is treated as an afterthought.</strong> Service-business schema (LocalBusiness, Service, FAQPage) is shallow or absent on Squarespace pages by default. Trade-specific structured data needs custom code injection, which Squarespace allows but does not encourage.</li>
      <li><strong>Templates are over-recognizable.</strong> Designers who work in Squarespace can spot a Squarespace site immediately. So can your competitors. So can Google's "low-uniqueness" signals over time.</li>
      <li><strong>Lock-in is total.</strong> Squarespace has no real export. Stop paying and the site is gone. There is no "switch hosts" path. Your domain is portable; the site is not.</li>
      <li><strong>Subscription compounds.</strong> $23&ndash;$65/month for the platform, plus add-ons (commerce tier, scheduling, advanced analytics). Five years at the Business plan is roughly $2,160. The same five years on the monthly custom plan is $10,500 but you also get unlimited content updates, support, hosting, and a site that actually ranks.</li>
      <li><strong>Forms route through Squarespace.</strong> Submissions go to a Squarespace inbox; integration with your real email or CRM requires their Premium plan. With Web3Forms on a custom-coded site, every submission lands directly in your inbox, no platform middleman.</li>
    </ul>

